{"@context":["https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant/ns/content/v1.jsonld",{"@base":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio/","folio-assistant-sci":"https://litlfred.github.io/folio-assistant-sci/v0/ns/"}],"@id":"papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-covalent-bond-shared-trefoil","@type":["folio-assistant-sci:Definition","doco:Section"],"label":"def:covalent-bond-shared-trefoil","kind":"definition","title":"Covalent bond as shared electrons","uses":["papers/quantum-observable-universe/blocks/def-molecular-braid"],"tags":["covalent-bond","hopf-link","electron-sharing"],"leanRef":"qou:QOU.Molecular.CovalentBond","text":"covalent-bond-shared-trefoil.md","leanSource":"covalent-bond-shared-trefoil.lean","provenance":"authored","html":"<a id=\"def:covalent-bond-shared-trefoil\"></a><p><em>A <strong>covalent bond of order <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">k</code></strong> between atoms <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">i</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j</code> is the Hopf linking of <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">k</code> electrons across the two nuclei. Specifically, let <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e_1, \\ldots, e_k</code> be electrons from atom <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">i</code>. Each electron is the word <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma_0\\sigma_1\\sigma_0</code> in its local <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">H_3(q)</code>, whose closure is the Hopf link.</em></p>\n<p><em>In the molecular braid, the covalent bond operator for bond <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">b</code> connecting atoms <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">i</code> and <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j</code> is:</em></p>\n<pre><code class=\"language-math math-display\">  L_b = \\prod_{e=1}^{k_b} \\sigma_{g_e(\\mathfrak{p}_j)}^{-1} \\cdot \\sigma_{g_e(\\mathfrak{p}_i)}^{-1}</code></pre>\n<p><em>where <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">g_e(\\mathfrak{p}_j)</code> is the generator index for the crossing between shared electron <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">e</code> and proton <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\mathfrak{p}_j</code> of atom <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">j</code>. Both crossings are <code class=\"language-math math-inline\">\\sigma^{-1}</code> (EM attraction), so each shared electron contributes two attractive crossings—one to each nucleus.</em></p>\n","source":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/molecular-construction/covalent-bond-shared-trefoil.md","heading":"Definition","number":"18.1.4","page":"b/def-covalent-bond-shared-trefoil","qa":{"counts":{"pass":81,"fail":0,"warn":0,"na":11,"unknown":0,"stale":66},"milnor":{"total":3,"notPassing":0}},"lean":{"expected":true,"ref":"qou:QOU.Molecular.CovalentBond","via":"sibling","path":"folio/quantum-observable-universe/molecular-construction/covalent-bond-shared-trefoil.lean","compiles":"unchecked","sorry":false,"sorryBasis":"text"}}
